nables Link Time Optimization (LTO), which allows the\n2795\t\t\tcompiler to optimize binaries globally.\n2796\t\n2797\t\t\tIf unsure, select LTO_NONE. Note that LTO is very resource-intensive\n2798\t\t\tso it's disabled by default.\n2799\t\n2800\tconfig LTO_NONE\n2801\t\tbool \"None\"\n2802\t\t---help---\n2803\t\t\tBuild the kernel normally, without Link Time Optimization (LTO).\n2804\t\n2805\tconfig LTO_FULL\n2806\t\tbool \"GNU Full LTO (EXPERIMENTAL)\"\n2807\t\tdepends on ARCH_TOOLCHAIN_GNU\n2808\t\t---help---\n2809\t\t\tLink time optimization is implemented as a GCC front end for a bytecode\n2810\t\t\tbytecode representation of GIMPLE that is emitted in special sections\n2811\t\t\tof .o files. Currently, LTO support is enabled in most ELF-based systems,\n2812\t\t\tas well as darwin, cygwin and mingw systems.\n2813\t\n2814\tconfig LTO_THIN\n2815\t\tbool \"Clang ThinLTO (EXPERIMENTAL)\"\n2816\t\tdepends on ARCH_TOOLCHAIN_CLANG\n2817\t\t---help---\n2818\t\t\tThis option enables Clang's ThinLTO, which allows for parallel\n2819\t\t\toptimization and faster incremental compiles compared to the", "is_error": false}
